  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       05 · ABOUT MICHAEL — Type A: Deep navy
  ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="bg-void">
    <div class="container mx-auto px-6 py-20 md:py-28 max-w-3xl">

      <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl md:text-4xl lg:text-[42px] text-gold-gradient leading-tight mb-10">
        About Michael
      </h2>

      <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-8 sm:gap-12 items-start mb-14">
        <div class="w-full max-w-[160px] sm:max-w-[180px] shrink-0">
          <div class="rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-parchment/8">
            <div class="aspect-[4/5] overflow-hidden">
              <Image
                src={michaelPortrait2}
                alt="Michael Kaplan"
                class="w-full h-full object-cover object-top"
                widths={[320, 400]}
                sizes="180px"
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="font-heading text-xl text-parchment/90 mb-1">Michael Kaplan</p>
          <p class="font-body text-sm text-parchment/50 mb-1">ASCHP Registered Wellness Counsellor</p>
          <p class="font-body text-xs text-parchment/35">ASCHP Reg. 10559 &middot; Yoga Alliance RYS 300</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="font-body text-lg md:text-xl text-parchment/68 leading-relaxed space-y-8">
        <p><strong class="text-parchment/85">On working with men of this generation.</strong></p>

        <p>I am of the generation I work with. I grew up with a phone in my hand, inside the same algorithmic culture, with the same fractured messages about what a man is supposed to be, and the same scarcity of older men available to help work any of it out.</p>

        <p>I mention this only because it matters in practice. The territory young men are navigating now is not abstract to me. I am not interpreting it from the outside.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-parchment/85">On the counselling and facilitation work.</strong></p>

        <p>I am registered with the ASCHP as a Wellness Counsellor. My approach combines reflective counselling with developmental mentorship, conversational and relational, grounded in careful attention to behaviour, emotional patterns, and the broader circumstances shaping a person's life.</p>

        <p>Much of my early work happened under the supervision of clinical psychologist Bernard Altman, and through several years inside a long-running mentorship organisation for young men, where I facilitated hundreds of group processes for adolescents and young adult men across very different social environments in Cape Town. That work involved psychodynamic group process, conflict, family dynamics, and the design of rites-of-passage experiences. It is where I learned, in practice, how young men actually open up, what they will and will not say in front of others, and what kinds of space allow honest reflection to happen at all.</p>

        <p>The goal of the work has never been to impose ideology or manufacture a result. It is to help someone develop greater clarity, steadiness, self-awareness, and ownership within the reality of their own life.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-parchment/85">On contemplative training.</strong></p>

        <p>I have spent nearly three years living and training in India within traditional contemplative settings, working with experienced teachers in meditation, pranayama, hatha yoga, and related disciplines.</p>

        <p>Where it is useful, aspects of this work, breath, attention, nervous system regulation, embodied awareness, are integrated into sessions in practical ways. Not as spiritual performance. As tools that help someone be more present, regulated, and capable in their own life. Many sessions involve none of this directly. The focus is always on what is actually useful for the person in front of me.</p>

        <p><strong class="text-parchment/85">On the approach overall.</strong></p>

        <p>I do not believe young men need to be shamed, hardened, or argued into a rigid model of masculinity to mature. I also do not believe every difficulty should be immediately treated as pathology.</p>

        <p>Most of the men I work with already know, somewhere in themselves, that something isn't working. What is usually missing is a space where they can think honestly, speak directly, and be met with both real understanding and real challenge, without either being softened away.</p>

        <p>That is the space this work exists to provide.</p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

